Cache syntax-highlight results and honour the fence's language tag

Problem
-------
`CodeBlockView` re-highlights on every `.onAppear`, and `HighlightTaskManager`
keeps no cache. In a virtualized transcript (`LazyVStack`) a code block is
recycled every time it scrolls back on screen, so the same block is tokenized
by highlight.js again and again.

It also discards the fenced language. `CodeBlockView` holds `language` and puts
it in the chrome label, but `updateAttributedString` calls the two-argument
`attributedText(_:colors:)` overload, which uses `mode: .automatic` —
highlight.js auto-detection across every bundled language.

Measured on an M-series Mac (release build, HighlightSwift at 99c431b), a
~20-line Swift block:

    attributedText(_:colors:)            .automatic        median 16.6 ms
    attributedText(_:language:colors:)   explicit "swift"  median  3.2 ms

    50 blocks, serialized, .automatic                      median  518 ms
    50 blocks, serialized, explicit language                median  116 ms

So auto-detection costs 5.2x, and one block exceeds a 60 Hz frame budget.

Fix
---
1. Forward `language` to the highlighter, falling back to `.automatic` when the
   fence carries no tag.
2. Cache results process-wide, keyed by (code, language, colors). Each
   `CodeBlockView` owns its own `HighlightTaskManager` in a `@StateObject`, and
   that state does not survive recycling, so the cache has to outlive the view.
   Results are immutable for a given key, so this is safe.

Uses the existing `@WithLock` wrapper rather than introducing new concurrency
primitives, matching `AppAppearance` and `ParagraphViewCache`.

